        ["During a graded walking test, a patient's systolic BP drops from 150 to 132 mmHg as the workload increases. How should you interpret this?",["An abnormal response; stop the test","A normal response to rising workload","A sign to increase the workload","A measurement error to be ignored"],"Systolic BP should rise with workload. A fall may indicate left ventricular dysfunction or ischemia and is a reason to stop."],
        ["A patient's BP is 128/78 mmHg supine and 104/70 mmHg after 1 minute of standing, and she feels lightheaded. What does this indicate?",["Orthostatic hypotension","A normal postural response","Hypertensive crisis","Autonomic dysreflexia"],"A systolic drop of 20 mmHg or more on standing, with symptoms, meets the definition of orthostatic hypotension."],
        ["In ideal standing posture viewed from the side, the plumb line passes through which landmarks?",["Ear lobe, acromion, and greater trochanter","Nose, sternum, and pubic symphysis","Mastoid, scapular spine, and ischial tuberosity","Ear lobe, olecranon, and medial malleolus"],"The plumb line runs through the ear lobe, acromion, greater trochanter, slightly anterior to the knee axis, and slightly anterior to the lateral malleolus."],
        ["Viewed from the side, a client's ear lobe sits well in front of the acromion and her shoulders are rounded. Which postural fault is this?",["Forward head posture","Flat-back posture","Lumbar hyperlordosis","Scoliosis"],"Forward head posture places the head anterior to the plumb line, which increases the load on the cervical extensors."],
        ["A well-trained 25-year-old distance runner has a resting heart rate of 48 bpm with no symptoms. What is the most likely interpretation?",["A normal training adaptation","A medical emergency needing EMS","An early sign of heart failure","A sign of hyperthyroidism"],"Endurance training increases stroke volume, so resting bradycardia without symptoms is common and benign in athletes."],
        ["A patient's pulse feels irregular. What is the best way to measure the heart rate?",["Count for a full 60 seconds","Count for 10 seconds and multiply by 6","Count for 15 seconds and multiply by 4","Estimate it from the patient's breathing rate"],"Short counts multiply the error when the rhythm is irregular, so a full 60-second count is more accurate."],
        ["You take a patient's BP with her arm hanging at her side instead of supported at heart level. How will this MOST likely affect the reading?",["It will read falsely high","It will read falsely low","It will not change the reading","It will change only the diastolic"],"With the arm below heart level, added hydrostatic pressure raises the reading. The arm should be supported at heart level."],
        ["A patient's SpO2 reads 84% on a cold, pale finger, yet she is talking comfortably, has a normal respiratory rate, and has no distress. What is the BEST next step?",["Warm the hand or move the probe and recheck","Call EMS immediately for severe hypoxemia","Ignore the reading and start the session","Have her hyperventilate to raise the number"],"Poor peripheral perfusion from cold hands commonly causes falsely low readings. Recheck with a warmer site before acting, while still watching for clinical signs of distress."],
        ["During a cycle ergometer test, a patient's BP rises from 130/80 at rest to 250/100 at a moderate workload. What is the BEST interpretation?",["A hypertensive response that is a reason to stop","A normal response to increasing exercise","A normal response because diastolic rose only slightly","A sign that the workload should be increased"],"Systolic pressure should rise gradually; a systolic above about 250 mmHg or diastolic above about 115 mmHg is a commonly cited ACSM criterion for stopping exercise."],
        ["Viewed from the side, a client's pelvis sits forward of the plumb line, her upper trunk leans back, and her hips are in extension. Which postural fault is this?",["Sway-back","Flat back","Lordotic","Kyphotic"],"Sway-back posture shifts the pelvis anteriorly with the trunk swayed posteriorly. Lordotic posture involves anterior pelvic tilt with increased lumbar curve."],
        ["Why should you count respiratory rate without telling the patient you are doing so?",["Patients tend to change their breathing when they know it is watched","Counting aloud increases the patient's heart rate during the count","Respiratory rate can only be measured while the patient is asleep","It allows you to skip measuring heart rate during the session"],"Awareness alters breathing rate and depth. Counting while appearing to take the pulse gives a more accurate resting value."]
